# ☁️ BASIC IT & CLOUD TERMS

IT (Information Technology) — Technology used to manage computers, software, networks, and data.
ICT (Information and Communication Technology) — Technology used for computing and digital communication.
HW (Hardware) — Physical components of a computer, such as CPU, RAM, and storage.
SW (Software) — Programs and applications that run on computers.
OS (Operating System) — Software that manages computer hardware and applications.
CPU (Central Processing Unit) — The main processor that executes instructions.
RAM (Random Access Memory) — Temporary memory used by running programs.
HDD (Hard Disk Drive) — Magnetic storage used to save data permanently.
SSD (Solid State Drive) — Fast electronic storage used to save data.
VM (Virtual Machine) — A software-based computer that runs inside a physical computer.
CLI (Command Line Interface) — A text-based way to interact with a computer using commands.
GUI (Graphical User Interface) — A visual way to interact with software using windows, icons, and buttons.
API (Application Programming Interface) — A way for two software applications to communicate.
SDK (Software Development Kit) — A collection of tools used to build applications.
IDE (Integrated Development Environment) — Software used to write, run, and debug code.
URL (Uniform Resource Locator) — The address of a resource on the internet.
URI (Uniform Resource Identifier) — A unique identifier for a resource.
IP (Internet Protocol) — Rules used to identify and communicate between devices on a network.
MAC (Media Access Control) — A unique hardware address assigned to a network interface.
DNS (Domain Name System) — Converts domain names into IP addresses.
ISP (Internet Service Provider) — A company that provides internet access.
HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) — Protocol used to transfer web data.
HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) — Secure and encrypted version of HTTP.
SSL (Secure Sockets Layer) — Older technology used to secure network communication.
TLS (Transport Layer Security) — Modern security protocol used to encrypt communication.
TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) — Reliable protocol used to transfer data.
UDP (User Datagram Protocol) — Fast protocol that transfers data without guaranteed delivery.
LAN (Local Area Network) — A network covering a small area.
WAN (Wide Area Network) — A network covering a large geographical area.
WLAN (Wireless Local Area Network) — A local network that uses wireless communication.
VPN (Virtual Private Network) — Creates a secure encrypted connection over the internet.

☁️ CLOUD COMPUTING TERMS
CC (Cloud Computing) — Delivery of computing services over the internet.
CSP (Cloud Service Provider) — A company that provides cloud computing services.
IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service) — Provides virtual servers, storage, and networking.
PaaS (Platform as a Service) — Provides a platform to develop and deploy applications.
SaaS (Software as a Service) — Provides ready-to-use software through the internet.
FaaS (Function as a Service) — Runs individual functions without managing servers.
BaaS (Backend as a Service) — Provides ready-made backend services for applications.
DBaaS (Database as a Service) — Provides managed database services in the cloud.
DaaS (Desktop as a Service) — Provides virtual desktops through the cloud.
XaaS (Everything as a Service) — General term for services delivered through the cloud.
PAYG (Pay As You Go) — Pay only for the cloud resources you use.
HA (High Availability) — Keeps applications available with minimum downtime.
FT (Fault Tolerance) — Allows a system to continue working when a component fails.
DR (Disaster Recovery) — Restores systems and data after a disaster.
BCP (Business Continuity Plan) — A plan to keep business operations running during disruptions.
RTO (Recovery Time Objective) — Maximum acceptable time to restore a system.
RPO (Recovery Point Objective) — Maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured by time.
SLA (Service Level Agreement) — Agreement that defines expected service performance and availability.
CAPEX (Capital Expenditure) — Money spent on purchasing physical infrastructure.
OPEX (Operational Expenditure) — Money spent on ongoing operational services.
TCO (Total Cost of Ownership) — Total cost of owning and operating a system.

🌐 CLOUD DEPLOYMENT MODELS
Public Cloud — Cloud infrastructure shared by multiple customers.
Private Cloud — Cloud infrastructure dedicated to one organization.
Hybrid Cloud — Combination of public and private cloud.
Multi-Cloud — Using services from multiple cloud providers.
On-Prem (On-Premises) — Infrastructure hosted inside an organization's own location.

Major CSPs include AWS, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.

🟠 AWS FULL FORMS – BASIC TO ADVANCED
AWS (Amazon Web Services) — A cloud computing platform that provides servers, storage, databases, networking, and other services.
EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud) — Provides virtual servers in AWS.
AMI (Amazon Machine Image) — A template used to create EC2 instances.
EBS (Elastic Block Store) — Block storage used with EC2.
S3 (Simple Storage Service) — Object storage used to store files and data.
EFS (Elastic File System) — Shared file storage for multiple servers.
RDS (Relational Database Service) — Managed relational database service.
DMS (Database Migration Service) — Helps migrate databases to AWS.
VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) — A private virtual network inside AWS.
IGW (Internet Gateway) — Connects a VPC to the internet.
NAT (Network Address Translation) — Allows private resources to access the internet.
NACL (Network Access Control List) — Controls network traffic at the subnet level.
SG (Security Group) — Virtual firewall that controls instance traffic.
ENI (Elastic Network Interface) — Virtual network interface used by AWS resources.
EIP (Elastic IP) — Static public IPv4 address.
ELB (Elastic Load Balancing) — Distributes traffic across multiple servers.
ALB (Application Load Balancer) — Load balancer for HTTP and HTTPS traffic.
NLB (Network Load Balancer) — High-performance load balancer for TCP and UDP traffic.
GWLB (Gateway Load Balancer) — Distributes traffic to virtual network appliances.
ASG (Auto Scaling Group) — Automatically manages the number of EC2 instances.
IAM (Identity and Access Management) — Controls users, roles, and permissions.
MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication) — Uses multiple verification methods for login.
STS (Security Token Service) — Provides temporary security credentials.
KMS (Key Management Service) — Creates and manages encryption keys.
ACM (AWS Certificate Manager) — Manages SSL/TLS certificates.
WAF (Web Application Firewall) — Protects web applications from common web attacks.
CDN (Content Delivery Network) — Delivers content from locations closer to users.
CF (CloudFormation) — AWS Infrastructure as Code service.
CW (CloudWatch) — Monitors AWS resources, logs, and metrics.
CT (CloudTrail) — Records AWS account activity and API calls.
SNS (Simple Notification Service) — Sends notifications using a publish/subscribe model.
SQS (Simple Queue Service) — Message queue service used to decouple applications.
SES (Simple Email Service) — Cloud-based email sending service.
ECS (Elastic Container Service) — AWS container management service.
EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service) — Managed Kubernetes service.
ECR (Elastic Container Registry) — Stores and manages container images.
Fargate — Serverless compute engine for containers.
Lambda — Serverless compute service that runs code without managing servers.
API Gateway — Service used to create and manage APIs.
Route 53 — AWS DNS and domain routing service.
AZ (Availability Zone) — One or more isolated data centers inside an AWS Region.
ARN (Amazon Resource Name) — Unique identifier for an AWS resource.

♾️ DEVOPS BASIC TERMS
DevOps (Development and Operations) — A culture and practice that combines development and operations to deliver software faster.
SDLC (Software Development Life Cycle) — Process used to plan, develop, test, deploy, and maintain software.
CI (Continuous Integration) — Developers frequently merge code and automatically test it.
CD (Continuous Delivery) — Code is automatically prepared for production deployment.
CD (Continuous Deployment) — Tested code is automatically deployed to production.
CI/CD (Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery/Deployment) — Automation of software build, test, and deployment.
SCM (Source Code Management) — Process of managing changes to source code.
VCS (Version Control System) — Software used to track code changes.
DVCS (Distributed Version Control System) — Version control where every developer has a complete repository copy.
IaC (Infrastructure as Code) — Managing infrastructure using code.
CaC (Configuration as Code) — Managing system configurations using code.
GitOps — Managing infrastructure and deployments using Git repositories as the source of truth.
DevSecOps (Development, Security, and Operations) — Integrates security into the DevOps process.
SRE (Site Reliability Engineering) — Uses software engineering practices to improve system reliability.
AIOps (Artificial Intelligence for IT Operations) — Uses AI to automate and improve IT operations.
MLOps (Machine Learning Operations) — Practices used to deploy and manage machine learning systems.
🔀 GIT & GITHUB TERMS

Git official site> and GitHub are core tools in many DevOps workflows.

Git — Distributed version control system used to track source code changes.
GitHub — Cloud platform used to host and collaborate on Git repositories.
Repo (Repository) — Storage location for project files and Git history.
WD (Working Directory) — Area where developers edit project files.
SA (Staging Area) — Temporary area that stores changes before a commit.
SHA (Secure Hash Algorithm) — Hash used by Git to identify objects and commits.
PR (Pull Request) — Request to review and merge code changes.
MR (Merge Request) — Another term for a request to merge code changes.
SSH (Secure Shell) — Secure protocol used to access remote systems.
PAT (Personal Access Token) — Token used for authentication.
HEAD — Pointer to the currently checked-out Git commit or branch.
CI/CD Pipeline — Automated sequence of build, test, and deployment steps.
🐳 DOCKER & CONTAINER TERMS
Docker — A platform used to build, package, and run applications in containers.
OCI (Open Container Initiative) — Creates open standards for container formats and runtimes.
CRI (Container Runtime Interface) — Interface used by Kubernetes to communicate with container runtimes.
CNI (Container Network Interface) — Standard used to configure container networking.
CSI (Container Storage Interface) — Standard used to connect storage systems with containers.
Dockerfile — File containing instructions to build a Docker image.
Image — Read-only template used to create containers.
Container — Lightweight isolated environment used to run applications.
Registry — Storage system for container images.
Docker Hub — Cloud registry for Docker images.
YAML (YAML Ain't Markup Language) — Human-readable configuration file format.
JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) — Data format used to store and exchange structured data.
☸️ KUBERNETES TERMS
K8s (Kubernetes) — Container orchestration platform used to manage containers.
K3s (Lightweight Kubernetes) — Lightweight Kubernetes distribution.
API Server (Application Programming Interface Server) — Main communication point of a Kubernetes cluster.
etcd — Distributed key-value database that stores Kubernetes cluster data.
CM (Controller Manager) — Manages Kubernetes controllers.
KCM (Kube Controller Manager) — Runs core Kubernetes control loops.
CRD (Custom Resource Definition) — Adds custom resources to Kubernetes.
RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) — Controls access based on roles.
PVC (Persistent Volume Claim) — Request for persistent storage.
PV (Persistent Volume) — Storage resource available to a Kubernetes cluster.
HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler) — Automatically changes the number of pods.
VPA (Vertical Pod Autoscaler) — Adjusts CPU and memory resources for pods.
PDB (Pod Disruption Budget) — Limits pod disruptions during maintenance.
DNS (Domain Name System) — Provides service name resolution inside a cluster.
Ingress — Manages external HTTP and HTTPS access to services.
