IAM (Identity and Access Management) — Manages identities and permissions.
AAA (Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting) — Security framework for verifying and tracking users.
RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) — Provides permissions based on roles.
ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control) — Provides access based on attributes.
MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication) — Uses multiple authentication methods.
SSO (Single Sign-On) — Allows users to access multiple systems with one login.
JWT (JSON Web Token) — Token format used for secure authentication.
OAuth (Open Authorization) — Authorization framework used to provide limited access.
OIDC (OpenID Connect) — Identity authentication layer built on OAuth 2.0.
AES (Advanced Encryption Standard) — Symmetric encryption algorithm.
RSA (Rivest-Shamir-Adleman) — Public-key encryption algorithm.
DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service) — Attack that floods a system with traffic.
WAF (Web Application Firewall) — Protects web applications from malicious traffic.
CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) — Public identifier for known security vulnerabilities.
CVSS (Common Vulnerability Scoring System) — Scores the severity of vulnerabilities.
OWASP (Open Worldwide Application Security Project) — Organization focused on web application security.
🚀 ADVANCED CLOUD & DEVOPS TERMS
SOA (Service-Oriented Architecture) — Architecture where applications are built using reusable services.
MSA (Microservices Architecture) — Architecture where an application is divided into small independent services.
REST (Representational State Transfer) — Architecture style used to design web APIs.
RPC (Remote Procedure Call) — Allows a program to execute a function on another system.
gRPC (Google Remote Procedure Call) — High-performance RPC framework.
MQ (Message Queue) — Allows asynchronous communication between applications.
DLQ (Dead Letter Queue) — Stores messages that failed processing.
EDA (Event-Driven Architecture) — Architecture where systems communicate using events.
CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) — Separates read and write operations.
CAP (Consistency, Availability, Partition Tolerance) — Distributed system principle describing system trade-offs.
ACID (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) — Properties that ensure reliable database transactions.
BASE (Basically Available, Soft State, Eventual Consistency) — Distributed database consistency model.
Blue-Green Deployment — Uses two environments to reduce deployment downtime.
Canary Deployment — Releases a new version to a small number of users first.
Rolling Deployment — Gradually replaces old application instances with new ones.
Zero Downtime Deployment (ZDD) — Deploys applications without service interruption.
RCA (Root Cause Analysis) — Process used to identify the main cause of a problem.
PoC (Proof of Concept) — Small implementation used to test whether an idea works.
MVP (Minimum Viable Product) — Basic product version with essential features.